# UI/UX — authoring doctrine

`context/instructions/ui.md` is the contract for any user-interface work:
layouts, components, colour, type, spacing, motion, copy and review. It is
project-agnostic — it names decisions, not frameworks or design systems. Read it
together with `context/instructions/development.md` (the process contract:
agreement, library-first, simplicity, surgical changes) and the references it
routes to. Concrete render verification is owned by
`context/instructions/browser.md`.

## When this applies (no-op rule)

- Use it when the project renders a user interface — a web page, a desktop or
  mobile app, a terminal UI, a settings screen. If the project has no user
  interface, this file does not apply: say so and stop.
- Everything here assumes the project's own stack, tokens and system, not a
  specific one. If a rule needs a design system the project does not have, do
  not invent one (see `ui-tokens.md`).
- For per-context layout and input (responsive / device adaptation) see
  `ui-adapters.md`; native iOS/Android playbooks are out of scope for this module.

## Priority ladder (tie-breaker)

When guidance conflicts, the higher tier wins:

1. **User needs** — does it help the person do the thing?
2. **Accessibility** — usable by keyboard, screen reader, low vision, motion
   sensitivity.
3. **Consistency** — with the rest of the product, not with a trend.
4. **Aesthetics** — visual direction and taste.
5. **Developer experience** — ergonomics of building it.

Taste serves tier 4 and never overrides accessibility.

## Visitor-success mode

Choose one mode from the **requested surface**, not the product; it sets the
register:

- **Persuade** — landing/marketing: distinctiveness and one clear message.
- **Operate** — tool/dashboard/settings: earned familiarity is the virtue;
  clarity beats novelty.
- **Read** — long-form: typography and rhythm serve comprehension.
- **Experience** — interactive/immersive: the interaction is the point, with its
  cost declared.

What reads as a cliché on a Persuade surface may be the correct convention on an
Operate one, so `ui-taste.md` does not apply uniformly across modes.

## Refine or redesign — classify first

- **Refine preserves; redesign replaces** — decide which was asked before
  touching anything.
- **Visual authority is evidence, not a filename**: a missing design-system
  document does not make a project greenfield, and the old look is evidence of the
  subject, not authority over it.
- On a redesign, routes/slugs, navigation labels, form field names and order, the
  logo, and legal/consent copy never change silently — they are contract, not
  style.

## The work loop

1. **Design read** — before generating, state in one short line how you read the
   request. Ask at most one clarifying question, and only when the read genuinely
   diverges; never ask for CSS values or offer canned aesthetic lanes.
2. **Build** — reuse the project's existing vocabulary and follow the decision
   reference for what you are changing (router below).
3. **Verify — bounded** — build fully, then inspect once in a single batched
   round (desktop and mobile together); fix everything in one batch; confirm with
   at most one more round; stop. Open-ended self-QA is waste.
4. **Close** — state the disposition and, if you degraded, what is unverified.

**Capture validity** — a capture is evidence only when it is opened and confirmed
against its name: no blank regions, sane dimensions, the document top where it is
claimed, entrance motion settled.

## State coverage

For every interactive element, enumerate the states —
default, hover, focus, active, disabled, loading, error, selected — render the
ones that apply, and say why the others do not. Focus-visible must exist for
keyboard users; never remove focus styling without an equally visible
replacement. The component reference (`ui-components.md`) details variants,
composition and overlays.

## Accessibility (P0)

Accessible by default, not as a later pass: a name and role for every control,
full keyboard operation, visible focus, sufficient contrast, adequate target
size, and motion that respects reduced-motion. This tier outranks aesthetics.
The full rules live in `ui-accessibility.md`.

## Truth vs demonstration

Commercial and factual claims — prices, customers, benchmarks, endpoints,
capabilities — are **uninventable**; do not fabricate them. Demonstration content
is authored at full fidelity and labelled synthetic — never leave placeholder
text as final, and never substitute chrome (a fake screenshot, a hand-rolled
icon) for real content.

## Verification and honesty

- **Never state a number you did not measure**; report only what a check produced.
- **Performance is part of done** — name the budget before building and measure it
  after (`context/instructions/ui-performance.md`).
- **Render and LOOK** — rules do not prove pixels; a layout claim needs a render
  (`context/instructions/browser.md`).
- **A skipped gate is not a pass** — say which claim is therefore unverified.
- **Self-review is not review** — the closing pass is fresh eyes with no inherited
  transcript; a silently degraded review has failed.
- Declare a degraded result; do not let it pass as complete.

## Which file to read

| Decision | Read |
|---|---|
| Colour, spacing, type, radius, motion tokens | `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md` |
| Building or changing a component | `context/instructions/ui-components.md` |
| Any interactive or visual change, plus review | `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md` |
| Layout, visual direction, copy, review | `context/instructions/ui-taste.md` |
| Arranging a surface, spacing and the type scale | `context/instructions/ui-layout.md` |
| Performance, budgets and measurement | `context/instructions/ui-performance.md` |
| Mapping a framework's idioms | `context/instructions/ui-adapters.md` |
| Rendering the result to verify it | `context/instructions/browser.md` |

## Boundary

- `development.md` owns the process: agree style/architecture first, library-first,
  simplicity, surgical changes. Where both apply, cite it; do not restate it.
- This module states design doctrine only. It prescribes no framework, no
  component library and no design system.
- Vector assets (icons, logos, illustrations) are owned by
  `context/instructions/vector.md`.
